that the Espionage Act

00:23:57.130 --> 00:24:00.430
is used against somebody who
is not a government employee

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:01.890
who does not have a security clearance.

00:24:01.890 --> 00:24:03.440
So what is going on here?

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:06.930
How is the US trying to
use the Espionage Act

00:24:06.930 --> 00:24:10.900
against an Australian who
didn't leak any information.

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:13.373
He published information
that was leaked to him.

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:17.613
(upbeat music)

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:23.760
So bucket two, I'm
calling aiding or helping.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:27.890
The US case here uses the
Espionage Act to say that, indeed,

00:24:27.890 --> 00:24:31.080
it was Chelsea Manning who
broke the law directly.

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:32.543
As an Army Intel Analyst.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:35.730
It was her who had access to
the secrets she leaked to them.

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:38.620
But in US federal law, we
have this thing that says

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:41.342
that if you aid someone
in committing a crime,

00:24:41.342 --> 00:24:43.480
you are punishable,

00:24:43.480 --> 00:24:45.800
kind of as if you've
broken the law yourself.

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:47.440
Yep got it right here.

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:51.283
Title 18 folks, Crimes
and Criminal Procedure,

00:24:52.550 --> 00:24:54.760
Whoever commits an offense
against the United States

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:57.640
or aids, abets, counsel's,
commands, induces,

00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:01.580
or procures its commission
is punishable as a principal.

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:03.310
If you helped out, you did it too.

00:25:03.310 --> 00:25:06.990
So, they used this law
to say, yeah, Julian,

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:08.810
you didn't do the hacking and leaking,

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:12.360
but like you helped you aided,
abetted, counseled, induced,

00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:15.360
or procured and willingly caused Manning

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:18.100
to do this because you
promised that you were going

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:19.560
to publish all this stuff.

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:22.030
So, that was sort of like
incentivizing Chelsea Manning

00:25:22.030 --> 00:25:22.863
to do this.

00:25:22.863 --> 00:25:24.860
And that is why you are being punished

00:25:24.860 --> 00:25:26.410
under the Espionage Act.

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:28.760
Wait, this is a giant deal.

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:29.593
This is a big deal.

00:25:29.593 --> 00:25:32.680
This logical chain that was just created.

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:36.370
Assange is being charged
with aiding Manning,

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:40.870
a convicted spy because
he published the things

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:43.650
that the spy gave to him, okay?

00:25:43.650 --> 00:25:44.870
File that away for a second.

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:46.860
Let's get onto the third bucket in here.

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:49.380
The third bucket I'm calling publishing.

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:51.620
Counts 15 through 17,

00:25:51.620 --> 00:25:53.130
and let's just read directly straight here

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:54.000
from the indictment.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.930
The government says that
Assange having unauthorized

00:25:56.930 --> 00:26:00.970
possession of all of these
reports from Afghanistan and Iraq

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:03.020
and the State Department,
et cetera, which again,

00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:05.990
he had sort of induced
Manning to give to him.

00:26:05.990 --> 00:26:09.190
They then specifically
allege that the crime here

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:11.410
is that having all of these documents,

00:26:11.410 --> 00:26:15.420
he then communicated those
documents to the world

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:17.700
by publishing them on the internet.

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:21.810
Whoa, the crime is publishing.

00:26:21.810 --> 00:26:24.920
That's the crime counts 15 through 17

00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:27.223
are that he published something.

00:26:28.436 --> 00:26:31.436
(suspenseful music)

00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:37.750
Okay, we're done with the legal talk here.

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:40.850
Let's talk about what this means.

00:26:40.850 --> 00:26:42.720
Back to our three buckets here.

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:45.350
The first one is the least scary to me.

00:26:45.350 --> 00:26:48.260
Did Assange try to help
Manning hack a password

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:49.890
so that he could remain anonymous?

00:26:49.890 --> 00:26:53.640
Maybe, the facts aren't super
robust here, but whatever,

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:55.370
if there is evidence for
that and he gets convicted,

00:26:55.370 --> 00:26:57.200
that's five years in prison, okay.

00:26:57.200 --> 00:27:00.800
It's these other two buckets
that used the Espionage Act.

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:04.150
Let's that Assange was aiding
Manning because he promised

00:27:04.150 --> 00:27:05.500
to publish the documents.

00:27:05.500 --> 00:27:07.930
And then he went further and
committed three more crimes

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:10.250
by actually publishing
them to all the world

00:27:10.250 --> 00:27:11.233
on the internet.

00:27:13.310 --> 00:27:15.703
Let me explain something
about journalism really quick,

00:27:16.690 --> 00:27:19.690
New York Times article about
Iran, doesn't matter, whatever.

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:21.120
When you read the international section

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.550
or the politics section
of any big newspaper,

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:27.730
you will see quotes from
officials inside the government

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:29.410
or diplomats or whatever,

00:27:29.410 --> 00:27:32.260
who request to remain anonymous,

00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:34.390
quote, because of the
sensitivity of the subject.

00:27:34.390 --> 00:27:36.270
What this means is some
government official talked

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:38.200
to the reporter at the New York Times

00:27:38.200 --> 00:27:40.717
and said, "Hey, I kind
of wanna remain anonymous

00:27:40.717 --> 00:27:42.657
"because I'm not really
supposed to be talking

00:27:42.657 --> 00:27:44.877
"to you about this but
I think the information

00:27:44.877 --> 00:27:47.257
"that I'm giving you is
for the public interest

00:27:47.257 --> 00:27:48.897
"and so I'm gonna do it anyway.

00:27:48.897 --> 00:27:50.400
"Please don't reveal my name."

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:52.510
And the New York Times
is like, okay, cool.

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:53.343
And they publish it.

00:27:53.343 --> 00:27:56.150
That happens every single
day all of the time.

00:27:56.150 --> 00:27:59.320
That is one of the most
important aspects of journalism

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:02.262
is that a journalist can have
a source who feels protected

00:28:02.262 --> 00:28:04.440
because they can remain anonymous

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:08.370
even if they talk about sensitive
or classified information.

00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:09.203
Why?

00:28:09.203 --> 00:28:12.580
Because in our society, we value the flow

00:28:12.580 --> 00:28:14.480
of important information for the public

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:17.330
even if the government has
deemed it to be classified.

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:19.210
So according to the indictment

00:28:19.210 --> 00:28:20.630
from the Department of Justice,

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:22.130
any New York Times article

00:28:22.130 --> 00:28:25.050
that is quoting a government
official who's talking

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:26.550
about classified information,

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:30.024
if the New York Times
publishes that, espionage.

00:28:30.024 --> 00:28:32.040
I mean, that's what they're saying here.

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:35.600
In fact tons of the leaks
that WikiLeaks published

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:40.490
in 2010 and 2011 were also
published by Der Spiegel

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:42.820
or the New York Times or the Guardian.

00:28:42.820 --> 00:28:45.440
Oh wait, but Julian
Assange isn't a journalist.

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:46.870
He's not like the New York Times reporter.

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:49.660
Those are like technically
legitimate journalist.

00:28:49.660 --> 00:28:50.590
No.

00:28:50.590 --> 00:28:53.810
Journalist is not a
legally defined status.

00:28:53.810 --> 00:28:55.950
Journalists don't enjoy
some upgraded version

00:28:55.950 --> 00:28:57.220
of free speech.

00:28:57.220 --> 00:29:00.750
All journalism takes place
within the free speech laws

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:02.630
that all of us enjoy.

00:29:02.630 --> 00:29:04.340
Oh, and I don't really need to say this,

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:06.090
but those laws, those protections

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:08.470
are really, really important.

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:11.640
Free speech is what keeps
our society transparent.

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:13.610
It keeps our government accountable.

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:15.690
It is important and it is rare.

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:16.523
It's a new thing.

00:29:16.523 --> 00:29:18.540
Like being able to hold
governments accountable

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:23.060
is like kind of a brand new
thing in human civilization.

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:24.027
It's not natural.

00:29:24.027 --> 00:29:25.500
It's not guaranteed.

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:26.670
It can easily go away.

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:30.240
So, that is why I'm
concerned about this case.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:31.073
Yeah, go ahead.

00:29:31.073 --> 00:29:33.670
Charge Julian Assange with the conspiracy

00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:35.420
to hack into a government computer.

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:37.640
But as soon as you
introduce the Espionage Act

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:40.590
into what he did, you
start to play with fire.

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:43.640
You're setting a precedent
that could easily be applied

00:29:43.640 --> 00:29:45.970
to the journalism that
we rely on every day

00:29:45.970 --> 00:29:47.460
to keep us informed

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:49.602
and to keep governments
and businesses accountable.

00:29:49.602 --> 00:29:52.750
Oh, and I'm not the only
one who's concerned by this.

00:29:52.750 --> 00:29:56.240
The Obama Administration also
worried that pursuing Assange

00:29:56.240 --> 00:29:59.789
would be dangerous for
free speech and journalism.

00:29:59.789 --> 00:30:01.190
So they didn't do it.

00:30:01.190 --> 00:30:03.870
It was the Trump Administration
that really doubled down

00:30:03.870 --> 00:30:06.310
and tried to get Assange and charge him.

00:30:06.310 --> 00:30:09.820
And I mean, are we surprised?

00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:12.120
So the Biden Administration
is sort of at a crossroads,

00:30:12.120 --> 00:30:13.060
like what are they gonna do?

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:14.981
Are they gonna push this?

00:30:14.981 --> 00:30:17.730
Are they gonna try to charge
Julian Assange with espionage?

00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:19.420
Well, it turns out they
